  <section class="section container">
    <h2>1. The three layers of a China-to-Bahrain cost — freight, statutory stack, and the island factor</h2>
    <p>
      “Shipping cost from China to Bahrain” is usually quoted as a single freight line, but the amount
      you actually pay is the sum of three groups. <strong>Group one</strong> is the international
      move: ocean freight for FCL/LCL or air freight by chargeable weight. <strong>Group two</strong>
      is the statutory stack: 5% customs duty on the CIF value, then 10% VAT on the duty-inclusive
      base — <code>CIF × 1.155</code> at the baseline. <strong>Group three</strong> is the
      destination fee stack: THC, documentation, customs brokerage, Bahrain processing and
      inspection, inland transport and, if free time is exceeded, demurrage or detention.
    </p>
    <p>
      Bahrain adds a fourth factor that the other Gulf lanes do not carry in the same way: island
      logistics. Every container that clears here funnels through one commercial gateway, and every
      Saudi-side movement crosses a bridge that is a border. That is the “island factor” — fewer
      competing paths, and more dependence on how one port and one crossing are running on the week
      your vessel arrives.
    </p>
    <p>
      The common mistake is to compare only group one. Two quotes can look identical on the ocean
      rate and diverge badly once THC, clearance and delivery are included — and on a small market
      like Bahrain, those destination lines are not absorbed by the scale that compresses fees in
      Dubai or Jeddah. This page keeps the three groups separate on every table so you can see which
      line a provider is actually quoting, and which lines are still missing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>2. Bahrain’s rate file: the February 2025 prints, flagged before you use them</h2>
      <p>
        Britain-sized lanes do not generate much public rate data, and Bahrain is the thinnest lane of
        the GCC six. The verified snapshot’s only sailing-freight prints for this route come from a
        forwarder sheet dated February 2025 — before the 2026 Gulf re-rating that pushed regional
        indexes to records. That makes them useful for shape and dangerous for budgeting. We publish
        them with the staleness attached rather than borrowing a Jebel Ali number and quietly
        renaming it.
      </p>
      <p>
        How far has the market moved? The SCFI’s Shanghai→Persian Gulf lane printed
        <strong>$6,311/TEU in September 2026</strong> (including surcharges), and Xeneta recorded
        China→Jeddah at <strong>$10,870/FEU, up 256% versus February 2026</strong> — above the
        Covid-era peak. Those are different bases from a port-to-port box quote, so do not convert
        them into a Bahrain price; read them as direction. The February 2025 Bahrain prints below
        sit in a market that no longer exists.
      </p>

      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'lane', label: 'Lane / size' },
          { key: 'benchmark', label: 'Published benchmark' },
          { key: 'read', label: 'How to read it' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={rateFileRows}
        caption="Every Bahrain FCL print in the verified snapshot is February-2025 vintage. LCL, air and DDP lines are request-for-quote items; the indicative bands shown are corridor snapshots, not Bahrain-verified numbers."
      />
      <SourceList sources={rateSources} title="Sources — Bahrain rate file" />

      <div class="note mt-2">
        <strong>Why we do not fill the gaps:</strong> on a lane this thin, a borrowed number is worse
        than an empty cell. A Dammam bracket would be wrong by the Hormuz premium; a Jebel Ali
        bracket would be wrong by schedule density. The correct substitute for missing data is a
        quote on your own volume — which is why section 7 turns this page’s gaps into a nine-line
        RFQ script.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>3. The ×1.155 stack, worked: 5% duty, then 10% VAT on the duty-inclusive base</h2>
    <p>
      Bahrain duty is calculated on CIF, so the first step is to build the CIF value correctly. CIF
      means the goods plus insurance plus freight. The second step is the baseline 5% customs duty on
      that value. The third step is Bahrain’s 10% VAT — applied on the duty-inclusive base, not on
      the CIF alone. The result is a compound multiplier, <code>CIF × 1.05 × 1.10 = CIF × 1.155</code>,
      which is half a percentage point different from a simple 15% — and that half-point is not
      rounding.
    </p>

    <h3>Step 1 — Build the CIF value</h3>
    <DataTable
      columns={[
        { key: 'layer', label: 'Cost layer' },
        { key: 'calc', label: 'How to obtain it' },
        { key: 'amount', label: 'Indicative amount' },
        { key: 'note', label: 'Planning note' },
        { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
      ]}
      rows={cifRows}
      caption="CIF $10,000 is an illustrative round figure only. Replace it with your actual declared value, freight and insurance."
    />
    <SourceList sources={taxSources} title="Sources — CIF and landed-cost layers" />

    <h3 class="mt-3">Step 2 — Duty, VAT, then destination fees</h3>
    <p>
      For a $10,000 CIF shipment, the 5% duty is $500, giving a duty-inclusive base of $10,500.
      Bahrain’s 10% VAT is $1,050, so the statutory landed cost is $11,550. Destination fees — THC,
      documentation, Bahrain clearance, inland transport and possible demurrage or detention — are
      added on top and were not quantified in the research snapshot, so request them itemised.
      There is also a timing nuance most quotes ignore: import VAT is normally payable before
      Customs releases the goods, so the $1,050 is a cash event at the port. VAT-registered
      importers in Bahrain can apply to the NBR for prior approval to defer it to their VAT return.
    </p>

    <div class="grid grid--2 mt-2">
      <Card title="The formula in one line">
        <p class="lead">
          <strong>CIF × (1 + duty rate) × (1 + VAT rate)</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          Baseline: CIF × 1.05 × 1.10 = CIF × 1.155. Add operational fees on top of that statutory
          figure, and check whether deferral is available to you.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card title="Where Bahrain sits in the GCC stack">
        <p>
          Saudi Arabia compounds to ×1.2075, the UAE and Oman to ×1.1025, and Qatar and Kuwait to
          ×1.05 with no VAT. Bahrain’s ×1.155 is the middle case — $525 above the UAE/Oman on a
          $10,000 CIF, and $525 below Saudi Arabia.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>

    <div class="note mt-2">
      <strong>Under-declaration is not a saving:</strong> the CIF value must reflect cost, insurance
      and freight. An unrealistically low invoice value invites revaluation, penalties and delay —
      and under the NBR’s import framework, a disputed VAT deposit is not quickly recovered.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>4. Khalifa bin Salman: what a one-port island does to the quote</h2>
      <p>
        Bahrain has one commercial container gateway that matters: Khalifa bin Salman Port, at Hidd,
        which took over the island’s seaborne trade when operations migrated from Mina Salman in
        2009. APM Terminals has operated the port since February 2009 under a long-running
        concession, and the terminal carries the trappings of a modern gateway — roughly 1,800
        metres of quay, 15 metres of alongside depth and capacity in the one-million-TEU class —
        but it is still a single gate on a small island.
      </p>
      <p>
        What does that mean for a buyer? First, <strong>no port arbitrage</strong>. In the UAE you can
        price Jebel Ali against Khalifa Port against Sharjah; in Saudi Arabia, Jeddah against
        Dammam. In Bahrain, every container-priced comparison collapses to one discharge point, so
        quotes differ on routing, service and fee schedule rather than on port choice. Second,
        <strong>less carrier competition per lane</strong>: smaller corridors support fewer strings,
        which is why schedule depth is thinner than Dubai and why a booking window here is a real
        line item, not a formality. Third, <strong>currency of information</strong>: when one port
        handles the market, the port’s status updates — closures, weather, congestion — travel
        straight into your delivery plan with no alternative to absorb them.
      </p>
      <p>
        The practical move is to treat the port question as settled and spend the comparison effort
        elsewhere: push on the destination fee schedule, ask which feeder or relay carries your box,
        and lock free time in writing. If you want a deeper port-level reference, see the
        <a href="/ports/khalifa-bin-salman/">Khalifa bin Salman port record</a> and the
        <a href="/middleeast/bahrain-ports/">Bahrain ports guide</a>.
      </p>

      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'node', label: 'Node' },
          { key: 'role', label: 'Role' },
          { key: 'note', label: 'Planning note' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={nodeRows}
        caption="Port/node orientation. China throughput figures and the Bahrain UN/LOCODE are HIGH confidence; destination operational details are LOW and route-specific."
      />
      <SourceList sources={nodeSources} title="Sources — ports & handlers" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>5. The King Fahd Causeway option: a Dammam-side door with a risk line</h2>
    <p>
      Bahrain’s geography gives it one genuinely distinctive card: the King Fahd Causeway, a 25 km
      series of bridges linking Al Jasra on the Bahrain side to Khobar on the Saudi side. It is the
      island’s only road link to the mainland, and it puts Bahrain within trucking range of Saudi
      Arabia’s Eastern Province — Khobar first, Dammam a short drive beyond, and the Dammam–Riyadh
      corridor from there. For importers whose ultimate market is eastern Saudi Arabia, that turns
      Bahrain from a small end-destination market into a possible alternate entry point when Jeddah
      is congested or Red Sea routing gets expensive.
    </p>
    <p>
      But read the causeway correctly. It is a <strong>border crossing, not a driveway</strong>. Cargo
      that lands in Bahrain and moves onward into Saudi Arabia meets Saudi import rules at the
      Saudi side — do not assume Bahrain’s ×1.155 stack applies to goods that are destined for
      Dammam. The crossing also has a security history: during the 2026 regional escalation, vehicle
      movement on the causeway was suspended as a precaution and reopened the same day in the
      reported case. Brief, reversible closures are exactly the kind of risk that should appear in
      a routing decision as a contingency line, not as a surprise demurrage bill.
    </p>

    <DataTable
      columns={[
        { key: 'route', label: 'Routing option' },
        { key: 'what', label: 'What it is' },
        { key: 'when', label: 'When it makes sense' },
        { key: 'watch', label: 'What to watch' },
        { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
      ]}
      rows={causewayRoutingRows}
      caption="Routing options are directional. Causeway-specific fees, clearance order and crossing times are not published in the verified snapshot — confirm with the carrier or broker before committing."
    />
    <SourceList sources={causewaySources} title="Sources — King Fahd Causeway routing" />

    <div class="note mt-2">
      <strong>How to price the option:</strong> ask for the Dammam + Causeway plan itemised — both
      clearances, the trailer legs on each side, crossing time, and a stated assumption for what
      happens if the bridge closes for a day. If a forwarder cannot itemise the dual-border steps,
      they are selling you a hope, not a route.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>6. Transit: the 22-day headline and the 15–30-day window</h2>
      <p>
        The China-to-Bahrain clock is a planning range, not a promise. WorldFreightHub route data
        gives Khalifa bin Salman a typical <strong>22 days</strong> with a 15–30 day range from
        Shanghai, Ningbo-Zhoushan and Shenzhen — all LOW confidence, because it is an estimate, not a
        carrier schedule. The window is 15 days wide, which on this lane is the number that should
        actually drive your inventory decision: the 30-day ceiling, not the 22-day midpoint, is what
        your warehouse and demurrage exposure should be built around.
      </p>
      <p>
        The one hard data point that keeps this honest is the February 2025 forwarder sheet, which
        paired Shenzhen with a 12–19 day transit. Either the lane has genuinely slowed since, or the
        2025 sheet described a best-case direct call that no longer exists in the current routing
        environment. Both readings end in the same place: verify the transit per booking, and treat
        any single number as this quarter’s version of the truth. Air and express timing for Bahrain
        was not published in the snapshot — do not assume the sea figure applies.
      </p>

      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'origin', label: 'Origin' },
          { key: 'mode', label: 'Mode' },
          { key: 'transit', label: 'Indicative transit' },
          { key: 'basis', label: 'Basis' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={transitRows}
        caption="Indicative transit windows only — verify with the relevant carrier before relying on these figures."
      />
      <SourceList sources={transitSources} title="Sources — China to Bahrain transit times" />

      <div class="note mt-2">
        <strong>Sea planning default:</strong> 22 days typical, 15–30 day range, from the three main
        China origins. Build the plan against the ceiling; treat the midpoint as the upside case.
        For air, express or Dammam-plus-Causeway moves, no verified timing was published — request a
        routing quote rather than extrapolating.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>7. Buying a thin lane: the RFQ script that closes the data gap</h2>
    <p>
      Here is the uncomfortable part of a lane like Bahrain: your published data is older than the
      market. The fix is not a better table — it is a better request. Treat the RFQ itself as
      research, and make nine specific asks. Each one exists because it is a place where a
      single-port, thin-data market can quietly move money between the quote you receive and the
      bill you eventually pay.
    </p>

    <ol>
      {rfqItems.map((item) => (
        <li>
          <strong>{item.ask}</strong> — {item.why}. <em>Watch out:</em> {item.watch}.
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>

    <div class="note mt-2">
      <strong>How to read the replies:</strong> if two forwarders answer these nine lines and their
      totals differ, the difference is almost never the ocean freight — it is the surcharge
      schedule, the free time, or the destination fee scope. That is exactly the money this page’s
      tables cannot show you, because Bahrain does not publish it. The RFQ is how you make the
      market reveal it.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>8. FCL, LCL or air: mode choice on a single-gateway lane</h2>
      <p>
        Mode choice on the Bahrain corridor follows the usual logic — sea for volume, air for urgency
        and value density — but the island setting sharpens one edge: LCL. A shared container is only
        as good as its consolidation volume, and Bahrain does not generate as much as Dubai. On a
        thin lane, an LCL consignment can sit longer waiting for its sailing, and the minimum charge
        bites harder at low volumes. That does not make LCL wrong for small shipments; it makes the
        CFS cutoff and the next consolidation date two of the most important numbers in the quote.
      </p>

