      caption="Cost factors are operational context. Only duty and VAT are verified rules — unquantified fees are LOW confidence and require a fee schedule."
    />
    <SourceList sources={[...modeSources, ...costSources]} title="Sources — cost & transit factors" />

    <h3 class="mt-3">The destination stack, line by line</h3>
    <DataTable
      columns={[
        { key: 'item', label: 'Cost component' },
        { key: 'who', label: 'Who charges it' },
        { key: 'magnitude', label: 'Indicative magnitude' },
        { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
      ]}
      rows={costRows}
      caption="Only the 5% duty and 10% VAT are stated from verified/secondary sources. Unquantified Bahrain fees are LOW confidence because the snapshot did not publish amounts."
    />
    <SourceList sources={taxSources} title="Sources — Bahrain destination fees & customs" />

    <h3 class="mt-3">Demurrage and detention</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Demurrage</strong> is charged by the terminal when an imported container stays inside
      the port beyond the free time allowed after discharge. <strong>Detention</strong> is charged
      by the ocean carrier when the container stays outside the port beyond the free time allowed
      after collection. On a single-port island, both clocks run in the same place: if Khalifa bin
      Salman is congested, there is no second terminal to reroute to, so the free time you negotiate
      is the only buffer you own. Free-time allowances and per-day rates differ by line and terminal
      and were not published in the snapshot — request the schedule before booking.
    </p>
    <p>
      The practical way to stay off both clocks is to finish clearance before the vessel arrives:
      file the Bahrain import declaration early, keep the HS code and documents ready, screen
      restricted goods, and book trucking before the container is discharged. If you are a
      VAT-registered importer, sort out the deferral approval in advance — an unsettled VAT position
      holds cargo just as effectively as an unpaid duty line.
    </p>

    <h3 class="mt-3">Cost discipline on a one-port lane</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Consolidate smaller orders into fewer, fuller FCL moves when your supplier allows it.</li>
      <li>Compare the FCL and LCL all-in quotes at the same destination point — not at different bases.</li>
      <li>Classify the HS code and screen restricted goods before booking.</li>
      <li>File the Bahrain import declaration before the vessel arrives.</li>
      <li>Book last-mile trucking before discharge to protect free time.</li>
      <li>Negotiate the destination fee schedule, not just the headline freight rate.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>10. Clearance, HS codes, and the SABER/SASO non-question</h2>

      <h3>Tax and duty</h3>
      <p>
        Bahrain’s baseline import duty is <strong>5% of CIF value</strong> under the GCC Common
        External Tariff, and its <strong>10% VAT</strong> is applied on the duty-inclusive base. That
        means the statutory landed cost for most goods is <code>CIF × 1.05 × 1.10 = CIF × 1.155</code>.
        Specific goods can carry different duty lines — alcohol, tobacco, paper and aluminium run
        higher; basic food and GCC-origin goods can be duty-free — so the HS code remains the first
        cost decision, and the first thing to verify before a quote is written.
      </p>

      <h3>Bahrain clearance order</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Classify the goods with the correct <strong>HS code</strong> before quoting or booking.</li>
        <li>Confirm importer registration, permits and any restricted-commodity approval before shipment.</li>
        <li>Prepare the commercial invoice, packing list, bill of lading or air waybill and certificate of origin.</li>
        <li>File the Bahrain import declaration through the Bahrain Customs electronic clearance system.</li>
        <li>Pay the assessed 5% duty and 10% VAT (or apply approved deferral), pass inspection if selected, and release the cargo.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Documents and classification</h3>
      <p>
        Standard documents are the commercial invoice, bill of lading or air waybill, packing list
        and certificate of origin, plus any product-specific permits for restricted goods. Classify
        with the correct <strong>HS code</strong> before quoting, because both duty and permit
        requirements depend on it. Note that <strong>ISF</strong> is a United States requirement and
        does not apply to Bahrain. One more boundary worth marking: Bahrain’s personal-parcel rules
        are not commercial import rules — personal shipments have their own threshold regime
        (refreshed in May 2026), and commercial consignments do not publish a de minimis at all.
        Confirm the commercial treatment with a licensed Bahrain broker.
      </p>

      <h3>No SABER or SASO for Bahrain</h3>
      <p>
        SABER and SASO are Saudi-only conformity systems and do not apply to Bahrain. Do not copy a
        Saudi SABER/SASO step into a Bahrain quote. Bahrain may have its own standards or
        product-approval requirements for certain goods, but those are not published in the verified
        snapshot — confirm the applicable Bahrain process for your product before shipment. If you
        also ship to Saudi Arabia, that is where the conformity talk belongs: see the
        <a href="/middleeast/gcc-customs-duty-comparison/">GCC customs and duty comparison</a> for
        how the two regimes diverge.
      </p>

      <h3>King Fahd Causeway border treatment</h3>
      <p>
        The Causeway is a real border, not a lane change. If your cargo crosses it toward Saudi
        Arabia, expect a second set of formalities on the Saudi side and confirm in advance which
        side clears the goods, under which regime, and at what fee. Causeway-specific clearance
        details, commercial timing and fee treatment are not published in the verified snapshot —
        treat any confident claim about them as LOW until your broker puts it in writing. For the
        wider picture on how these border rules compare across the Gulf, the
        <a href="/middleeast/shipping-from-china-to-bahrain/">main China-to-Bahrain guide</a> and
        the <a href="/middleeast/bahrain-customs-duty/">Bahrain customs and duty page</a> go deeper.
      </p>

      <SourceList sources={complianceSources} title="Sources — Bahrain customs, tax & ports" />
    </div>
  </section>

