  <section class="section container">
    <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>The stack is ×1.155, not 15%: 5% duty on $10,000 CIF is $500, then 10% VAT on the duty-inclusive $10,500 is $1,050, for $11,550 landed. The naive 5+10=15% shorthand gives $11,500 — the missing $50 is VAT charged on the duty.</li>
      <li>Freight is taxed on this lane: each extra $1 of freight adds $0.05 duty plus $0.105 VAT, or $0.155 all-in, so a $500 higher freight bill becomes a $577.50 landed increase. Comparing quotes on freight alone understates the real gap between them.</li>
      <li>Bahrain sits mid-stack in the GCC: ×1.155 lands $525 above the UAE/Oman ×1.1025 ($11,550 vs $11,025) and $525 below Saudi Arabia’s ×1.2075 ($12,075) on the same $10,000 CIF — the second-highest VAT load among the six, at half of Saudi’s rate.</li>
      <li>The only published FCL prints are February-2025 vintage — $950–$1,250 per 20ft and $1,200–$1,800 per 40ft from Shanghai; $800–$1,550 and $1,400–$2,350 from Shenzhen — flagged LOW and almost certainly understated while regional Gulf indexes sit at records.</li>
      <li>The 22-day typical sits inside a 15-day-wide window; the 30-day ceiling — not the midpoint — is the number to build inventory and demurrage exposure around. And the Causeway is an option with a risk line: it is the island’s only road link to Saudi Arabia, and it briefly closed during the 2026 escalation. Price a contingency, not just a crossing time.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
    <p><strong>Position:</strong> In my view, the only number on this lane I would trust without qualification is the ×1.155 stack — every freight figure deserves an asterisk, and I would rather quote Bahrain from a fresh RFQ built on nine hard questions than from any February-2025 print dressed up as a market rate.</p>
    <ul><li>The statutory stack is fixed and knowable: $10,000 CIF clears at $11,550, and each extra $1 of freight adds $0.155 — those two facts survive every data gap on this page.</li><li>The published FCL prints are 19 months old in a market where the SCFI’s Gulf lane hit $6,311/TEU and Xeneta’s China→Jeddah print rose 256% in seven months; using them as anything but a floor is a planning error.</li><li>A single-port island removes the port-choice question and amplifies everything else — fee schedules, free time and the Causeway’s closure risk are where this lane’s money actually hides.</li></ul>
    <p class="note"><em>Disclosure: This is the author's editorial judgment based on the published data on this page — not an independently verified fact.</em></p>
  </section>

